
*Blast Radius Side Panel*
- What it gives: When you're looking at the topology with highlighted nodes, you see incident context inline — severity, affected devices count, timeline — without switching back to the incidents page.
- Who benefits: NOC operators triaging live incidents. They stay on one screen instead of flipping tabs.
- Gain: Cuts mean-time-to-diagnose. Right now the flow is "see highlight → wonder what incident → go back → read → come back". Side panel removes that loop.

*Health History Timeline*
- What it gives: Trend data per node — was this device green all day or did it start flashing red 20 minutes ago?
- Who benefits: Network engineers doing root cause analysis. Managers tracking reliability.
- Gain: Answers "is this flapping?" and "did this start after the last change window?" Without it, health is a useless snapshot — you can't distinguish a transient blip from a degrading trend.

*Performance* (1000+ nodes)

- What it gives: The app doesn't crash or freeze on real-world networks.
- Who benefits: Every single user with a network >200 devices — i.e., your actual target audience.
- Gain: It's not a feature, it's a gate. Without it, the topology page is a demo toy, not a production tool.
Summary: Placeholder pages = cosmetic. These three = actual operational value. Pick one and we'll go.